ion means a plain index on `users.email` can't be used. The database has to scan the whole `users` table, applying `lower()` to every row, before it joins to `orders`. That cost grows with the table, so a query that was fine can drift into seconds as `users` grows. I only have the query, not the schema or a query plan, so this is an inference. Here's how I'd check and fix it:\n\n1. **Confirm with the plan.** Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. If you see a `Seq Scan on users` with a filter on `lower(email)`, that's the cause. Then check `orders` too, because a seq scan there would point to the next problem.\n\n2. **Fix the lookup.** Pick one:\n   - **Expression index** (least invasive): `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));` The query stays as it is.\n   - **Normalize on write**, with a `citext` column or by storing emails lowercased. Then query `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a normal index. This is cleaner long-term.\n\n3. **Index the join column.** Make sure `orders.user_id` has an index. Postgres doesn't create one automatically for foreign keys. Without it, the join is slow even after the user lookup is fast.\n\n4. **Replace `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide text or JSON columns, and it blocks index-only scans. List only the columns the page renders.\n\n5. **Check what changed last week.** The query text may be old. Look for a deploy that added `lower()`, a dropped or rebuilt index, a big data import, or stale statistics (`ANALYZE users, orders;`). The \"since last week\" timing matters, and `git log` here shows only one commit, so it doesn't tell me.\n\n6. **Check the app side.** If the page runs this query once per order (N+1) or fires it repeatedly, the query itself may be fast and the page still slow.\n\nIf you share the schema and the `EXPLAIN` output, I can point to the exact bottleneck. If the DB isn't Postgres, the syntax differs, but the reasoning is the same.", "